{"author":{"id":"f4e4dbe251fbd7519eab696986bae317e93a786739d6cebe9c4c1e89dd44e465"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793607858,"nonce":"7IkxPbipSH6Wlj4zWqU1oKC9Q38=","message":"Decided in the 2026-11-02 document review (design §11 updated to match).\n\n- **Recursion.** Structural recursion moves here from M4, next to well-founded recursion with `Spec.Decreases`.\n- **Equation lemmas.** For every recursive translated definition, the translator generates unfolding equation lemmas and proves them; the kernel checks them. In Lean these come from the elaborator's PreDefinition code, not `src/Lean/Meta/`, so porting the tactics does not supply them. `simp`/`unfold` use them, and so does the evaluator for differential tests of well-founded units, since kernel whnf barely reduces well-founded definitions.\n- **Exit criterion gains:** differential tests pass for at least one well-founded recursive unit, evaluated by rewriting with its equation lemmas.\n- **Sidecar scope.** The M5a attribute sidecar covers Init and the semantics library only. Std is exported and kernel-checked as M6's first step, and the sidecar is extended to Std there.\n- **Before M5a closes:** the Ansatz/arena cross-check (`d9535841280c65d90e8230f92e9a7f7d401e9a3f24c18bad3dde94ab280da37c`) and the §10.3 property tests (`338bdc941f0abc4450624a8751425fad4d2ce560a7fb9015ea2bfee89dad195b`) are done. Design §9 relies on cross-kernel agreement, and M5a produces the first certificates for translated code.","files":null}]}